    "body": "Uptime: running about 107h since 19:03 UTC Oct 17. Kills since last report: 00:54:56 and 04:12:52 Oct 22 (24h total to 04:12 is 26 restarts caused, per ledger). Last full machine reboot 20:15 Oct 20; next due about 08:15 UTC Oct 22.\n\nLedger since last report (00:53 UTC):\n- HTTP: lost 0 (0 since start across 26 restarts), accepted 177.09M, lost_within_flush_window_of_kill 0, stuck_over_24h 0. Oldest outstanding job 101 s.\n- WebSocket lost_commands: 6,092 -\u003e 7,801 (+1,709, PROBLEM). All in the 04:00Z hour. Still 7,801 at 06:44, so no loss since 04:59.\n- messages_after_expiry 40,605 -\u003e 58,605 (+18,000). messages_for_unknown_commands 0, replay_gaps_409 0, unrecorded_commands 1,337 -\u003e 1,475 (kill and down-slot window, expected category).\n- duplicate_events_after_resend 3.78M -\u003e 4.12M. Webhooks: duplicates 9,259 -\u003e 9,917 (+501 in the 04:00 hour, within 10m of the 04:12 kill; proportionate to earlier kills).\n\n04:00-04:40 trace: WS ramp (800-session step, ceiling 200) overlapping the 04:00 HTTP burst; kill 04:12:52; WS upstream dropping at 04:23; 1,000-session burst at 04:30. WS command p50 rose to 234 s and p99 to 346 s by 04:35. HTTP e2e p99 reached about 50 s. HTTP accept p99 stayed under 200 ms.\n\nUnexplained / caveats:\n1. Logs hold only about 500 lines (from 06:30), so I cannot match command IDs to 04:00. The 18,000 late messages are larger than the 1,709 lost, so many \"lost\" commands may have completed after the 5-minute expiry. The counter still counts them as lost.\n2. Redix \"event store unavailable\" timeouts crash WebSocketSessionRegistry GenServers mid-call (06:00:06-06:00:07 burst, :infinity GenServer.call). The lost counter did not move at 06:00.\n3. Open soak-alert #66 describes 311 lost HTTP jobs that this ledger does not show. Still unresolved.\n\nTrends (daily medians of hourly values, Oct 17-22):\n- open_fds median 1018, 1031, 1090, 1144, 1202, 1256 (partial day). Daily max 1059, 1111, 1233, 1294, 1278, 1302. Up about 45-55 per day for five days under the same load pattern. Kills reset the process, so a per-process leak should reset too. This is a warning-level leak signal.\n- RSS daily max 1031, 1199, 1098, 1155, 1193, 1016 MB. Plateau.\n- Pebble end median 1098, 1178, 1290, 1257, 1285, 1272 MB; files max 50-59. Bounded.\n- Accept p99 median 105, 125, 134, 148, 159, 172 ms. Slow steady drift, still far below 1 s.\n- Heap and goroutines still read 0 (unmeasured), so the fd climb cannot be checked against them.\n- WS command p99 hourly 40-300 s with steady-minute p50 about 1.4 s. Still unexplained.\n- Ramp ceilings: WS 400 (Oct 18), 200 (Oct 20), 200 (Oct 22, 400 step not sustained). Two WS ceilings at 200 in a row, but both ramps ran with HTTP bursts and upstream degradation, so this is confounded. HTTP ceiling 500 (Oct 21).\n\nHarness: healthy. All 107 hourly summaries present. minutes_target_unreachable only in kill hours. No generator_saturated flag, no driver restart gaps.\n\nNext:\n1. Keep logs through the 04:00 Oct 24 WS ramp (odd day) and the 08:15 reboot. Match new lost command IDs to those windows.\n2. Add a per-command expiry record, or a late-completion count, so \"lost\" can be separated from \"completed after 5 minutes\".\n3. Export heap and goroutine metrics. Check whether open_fds falls back to baseline after kills.\n4. Rerun a clean WS ramp with no HTTP burst and no upstream mode change."
